rules_version = '2';

// Chokro — Cloud Storage (SEC-6).
//
// ## EVERY CLIENT PATH IS DENIED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FILE
//
// One bucket, one writer, one reader. `server/src/reportJobs.js` is the only
// thing in this repository that puts an object here, and it does so through
// the Admin SDK, which bypasses these rules entirely. The only way a person
// reads one back is `POST /epr/reports/{jobId}/download`, which checks the
// caller's org membership, re-checks the report type's `minRole`, writes an
// audit entry, and mints a signed URL that expires in ten minutes.
//
// So a client has no legitimate reason to address this bucket directly. The
// Flutter app never does — it holds no `firebase_storage` dependency at all,
// and its photographs go to Cloudinary.
//
// ## WHY THE FILE EXISTS AT ALL, GIVEN IT ONLY SAYS NO
//
// It did not, and that was the finding. `firebase.json` declared no `storage`
// target, so `firebase deploy` never deployed a ruleset and nothing in the
// repository stated what the bucket's posture was supposed to be. The bucket
// holds every organisation's report artefacts, including the chain-of-custody
// export — the one row-level artefact in the product, carrying a bin and a
// date per disposal.
//
// An undeclared posture is not a safe one. It is an unknown one, and the
// difference matters most on the bucket nobody thought to check.
//
// ## IF THIS EVER NEEDS TO CHANGE
//
// It should not. A client that needs an artefact should get a signed URL from
// the route, because that route is where the role check and the audit entry
// live. Granting a read here would move the decision to a place that cannot
// log it.

service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
